{"input_text": "Question: I would like to calculate the following limit: $$\lim_ {n \to \infty} {\left( {n\cdot \sin{\frac{1}{n}}} \right)^{n^2}}$$ 
 or prove that it does not exist. Now I know the result is $\frac{1}{\sqrt[6]{e}}$, but I am having trouble getting to it. Any ideas would be greatly appreciated. 

Answer: $$
\left( {n\cdot \sin{\frac{1}{n}}} \right)^{n^2}
= e^{n^2 \log (n\sin(1/n))}
$$
now
$$
\log (\sin(x)/x) = \log\frac{x - x^3/6 + o(x^3)}{x}
= \log(1-x^2/6+o(x^2)) = -x^2/6 + o(x^2)
$$
hence
$$
e^{n^2 \log (n\sin(1/n))} = e^{n^2 (-\frac{1}{6n^2}+o(1/n^2))} = e^{-1/6 + o(1)} \to e^{-1/6}
$$ 
